NAME
       vgcreate - create an LVM volume group from physical volumes

SYNOPSIS
       vgcreate [-s EXTENT-SIZE] [-y] [-f] VG-NAME PV [PV...]

DESCRIPTION
       A volume group (VG) pools the space of one or more physical volumes
       (PVs: whole disks or partitions prepared for LVM) into one store, from
       which logical volumes (LVs) are then cut with lvcreate.

       The space of a VG is counted in physical extents (PEs), all of the
       same size: 4 MiB unless -s says otherwise. Every LV is a whole number
       of extents, so sizes round up to a multiple of the extent size, and
       the extent size cannot be changed later. When a task asks for LVs made
       of a number of extents (lvcreate -l 20), the extent size of the VG
       decides how big they are.

       Each PV argument may be a device that is already a PV, or a device
       that is not yet one: vgcreate then runs the pvcreate step itself.

       The new VG's device directory is /dev/VG-NAME.

OPTIONS
       -s, --physicalextentsize SIZE
              The extent size, a power of 2 such as 8M, 16M or 32M (units k,
              m, g; without a unit, MiB).

       -y, --yes
              Answer yes to questions (for example, to wipe an old file system
              signature on a device).

       -f, --force
              Go ahead without some checks.

       -v, --verbose
              Say more about what is done.

EXAMPLES
       Make a partition on /dev/vdb, turn it into a PV and create the VG
       vgdata with 16 MiB extents:

           parted -s /dev/vdb mklabel gpt mkpart lvm 1MiB 2GiB set 1 lvm on
           pvcreate /dev/vdb1
           vgcreate -s 16M vgdata /dev/vdb1

       Create a VG from two whole disks:

           vgcreate vgshare /dev/vdb /dev/vdc

       Check the extent size and free space:

           vgs -o vg_name,vg_extent_size,vg_size,vg_free vgdata
           vgdisplay vgdata

NOTES
       A device that holds a partition table or a file system is refused (or
       vgcreate asks before wiping its signature). Use an empty disk or a new
       partition, or clear it first with wipefs -a.

SEE ALSO
       pvcreate(8), vgextend(8), vgs(8), vgdisplay(8), lvcreate(8),
       vgremove(8), lvm(8)
